.TH "vsearch-udb" "5" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
udb \[em] a binary file format containing fasta sequences and a k-mer
index
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The UDB (USEARCH database) format is a binary file format used to store
fasta sequences together with a pre-computed k-mer index.
Building a UDB file from a fasta reference database with
\f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] allows subsequent search commands (such as
\f[V]--usearch_global\f[R]) to load the index directly, avoiding the
cost of recomputing it at each run.
.PP
Multi-byte numeric values are stored in the machine\[cq]s
\f[I]native\f[R] byte order; every platform vsearch currently supports
is \f[I]little-endian\f[R] (least significant byte first, as on x86 and
ARM processors), so in practice UDB files are little-endian and are not
portable to big-endian machines.
This contrasts with \f[I]big-endian\f[R] order (most significant byte
first), which is used for example by the SFF format (see
\f[V]vsearch-sff(5)\f[R]).
.PP
UDB files cannot be read from pipes; a seekable file path must be
provided.
.PP
The file consists of nine sequential sections described below.
.SS Section 1 \[em] Main Header
.PP
The main header is exactly 200 bytes long (50 × \f[V]uint32_t\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 buffer[0]   uint32_t   magic number: 0x55444246 (\[dq]UDBF\[dq])
 buffer[1]   uint32_t   reserved, set to 0
 buffer[2]   uint32_t   sequence index bits: 32
 buffer[3]   uint32_t   reserved, set to 0
 buffer[4]   uint32_t   word length (k-mer size, default 8)
 buffer[5]   uint32_t   dbstep: 1
 buffer[6]   uint32_t   dbaccelpct: 100
 buffer[7]   uint32_t   reserved, set to 0
 ...
 buffer[11]  uint32_t   slots: 0
 buffer[12]  uint32_t   reserved, set to 0
 buffer[13]  uint32_t   number of sequences
 buffer[14]  uint32_t   reserved, set to 0
 ...
 buffer[17]  uint32_t   alphabet: 0x0000746e (\[dq]nt\[dq])
 buffer[18]  uint32_t   reserved, set to 0
 ...
 buffer[49]  uint32_t   end marker: 0x55444266 (\[dq]UDBf\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
All fields not listed explicitly are reserved and set to zero.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]magic number\f[R] field value is 0x55444246.
Stored little-endian, its on-disk bytes read \[lq]FBDU\[rq]; the value
spells \[lq]UDBF\[rq] when written most significant byte first.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]word length\f[R] field stores the k-mer size used to build the
index (between 3 and 15, default 8; controllable with
\f[V]--wordlength\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]dbstep\f[R] field is always 1 and the \f[V]dbaccelpct\f[R]
field is always 100, reflecting that every sequence is indexed with full
coverage.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]number of sequences\f[R] field gives the total number of
sequences stored in the file.
It is never zero: vsearch rejects a UDB file declaring no sequence when
reading it, and refuses to write one.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]alphabet\f[R] field value is 0x0000746e, the little-endian
encoding of the ASCII string \[lq]nt\[rq] (nucleotide), indicating that
this is a nucleotide database.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]end marker\f[R] field value is 0x55444266 (on-disk bytes
\[lq]fBDU\[rq]; \[lq]UDBf\[rq] most significant byte first), marking the
end of the main header.
.SS Section 2 \[em] Word Match Counts
.PP
This section contains \f[V]4\[ha]wordlength\f[R] consecutive
\f[V]uint32_t\f[R] values (one per possible k-mer):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 kmercount[0]                uint32_t
 kmercount[1]                uint32_t
 ...
 kmercount[4\[ha]wordlength - 1] uint32_t
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each value \f[V]kmercount[i]\f[R] stores the number of sequences in the
database that contain k-mer \f[V]i\f[R] at least once.
With the default word length of 8, this section contains 65,536 values
(256 KB).
.SS Section 3 \[em] UDB3 Marker
.PP
A single \f[V]uint32_t\f[R] sentinel value:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 marker   uint32_t   0x55444233 (\[dq]UDB3\[dq])
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The value 0x55444233 is the little-endian encoding of the ASCII string
\[lq]UDB3\[rq].
.SS Section 4 \[em] Word Index
.PP
This section stores, for each k-mer, the sorted list of 0-based sequence
numbers of all sequences containing that k-mer.
The lists are concatenated in k-mer order, with no delimiters:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 seqno[0]   uint32_t   first sequence number for k-mer 0
 seqno[1]   uint32_t   second sequence number for k-mer 0
 ...        (kmercount[0] entries for k-mer 0)
 seqno[.]   uint32_t   first sequence number for k-mer 1
 ...        (kmercount[1] entries for k-mer 1)
 ...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
The total number of \f[V]uint32_t\f[R] values in this section equals the
sum of all \f[V]kmercount\f[R] values from section 2.
A k-mer with a count of zero contributes no bytes to this section.
Sequence numbers use 0-based indexing.
.SS Section 5 \[em] UDB4 Header
.PP
A fixed-size block of 32 bytes (8 × \f[V]uint32_t\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 buffer[0]   uint32_t   magic number: 0x55444234 (\[dq]UDB4\[dq])
 buffer[1]   uint32_t   constant: 0x005e0db3
 buffer[2]   uint32_t   number of sequences
 buffer[3]   uint32_t   total nucleotide count (low 32 bits)
 buffer[4]   uint32_t   total nucleotide count (high 32 bits)
 buffer[5]   uint32_t   total header characters (low 32 bits)
 buffer[6]   uint32_t   total header characters (high 32 bits)
 buffer[7]   uint32_t   constant: 0x005e0db4
\f[R]
.fi
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]magic number\f[R] field value is 0x55444234, the little-endian
encoding of the ASCII string \[lq]UDB4\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]number of sequences\f[R] field duplicates the value from the
main header.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]total nucleotide count\f[R] is a \f[V]uint64_t\f[R] stored as
two consecutive \f[V]uint32_t\f[R] values (low word first).
It gives the total number of bases across all sequences, which equals
the size of section 9.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]total header characters\f[R] is a \f[V]uint64_t\f[R] stored as
two consecutive \f[V]uint32_t\f[R] values (low word first).
It gives the total number of bytes in section 7, including one null
terminator per sequence.
.SS Section 6 \[em] Header Index
.PP
This section contains \f[V]seqcount\f[R] consecutive \f[V]uint32_t\f[R]
values:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 offset[0]            uint32_t   byte offset of sequence 0 header
 offset[1]            uint32_t   byte offset of sequence 1 header
 ...
 offset[seqcount-1]   uint32_t   byte offset of last sequence header
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each value is the byte offset of the corresponding sequence\[cq]s header
string within section 7.
Offsets use 0-based indexing relative to the start of section 7.
.SS Section 7 \[em] Headers
.PP
This section contains \f[V]header_characters\f[R] bytes: the ASCII
sequence headers concatenated in order, each terminated by a null byte
(\f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]).
The section is not padded.
There are no `>' characters; the header strings correspond to the part
of fasta header lines after the leading `>'.
.SS Section 8 \[em] Sequence Lengths
.PP
This section contains \f[V]seqcount\f[R] consecutive \f[V]uint32_t\f[R]
values:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 length[0]            uint32_t   length of sequence 0
 length[1]            uint32_t   length of sequence 1
 ...
 length[seqcount-1]   uint32_t   length of last sequence
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each value is the number of bases in the corresponding sequence.
.SS Section 9 \[em] Sequences
.PP
This section contains \f[V]ntcount\f[R] bytes: the ASCII nucleotide
sequences concatenated in order.
The section is not null-terminated and not padded.
Sequences can contain uppercase and lowercase letters (soft masking is
preserved).
T and U are treated as equivalent by vsearch.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Build a UDB file from a fasta reference database:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --makeudb_usearch db.fasta \[rs]
    --output db.udb
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Use a UDB file to search query sequences against the indexed database:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --usearch_global queries.fasta \[rs]
    --db db.udb \[rs]
    --id 0.97 \[rs]
    --blast6out results.tsv
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Inspect the content of a UDB file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch --udbinfo db.udb
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Extract the sequences stored in a UDB file back to fasta:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch --udb2fasta db.udb --output db_extracted.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
